What was reused, from files that are part of that MIT project:

- App categories messaging, email, and other, from AppDetectorHelper.kt.
- The messaging point rubric, the email checklist, and the general scam
  prompt, adapted into scam-guardian/engine.js. The adaptation keeps the
  scope to scam and phishing text. It does not copy the 0.85 and 0.15
  figures in ScamDetectionModelHelper.kt, because those numbers are fixed
  in the parser and are not a measured probability.
- The idea of reading a screenshot on the device, then judging the text.
  This page uses the already vendored Tesseract.js files for that reading.
  Scram's Android code, ML Kit, and screen-capture service are not included.

What was not copied, after reading the license and the tree:

- Gemma 3n weights. They are not in the Scram repository. Scram's README
  describes a 3.1 GB model under Google's Gemma terms, which are not MIT.
- App icons, Gradle files, and the empty GemmaLlmHelper.kt.
- TECHNICAL.md performance figures. They are the author's description of
  the Android app. This toolkit does not repeat them as its own results.

The optional browser model in this toolkit is a different, smaller Gemma
build. Its terms are separate from the MIT license above and are shown on
the page before download.
